**Diagram to draw:** a large **vertical section (V.S.) through the mammalian skin**, with the title "Vertical section of mammalian skin".

What to draw, from top to bottom:

- **Epidermis** (top layer): an outer thin **cornified (horny) layer** of dead cells, and under it the **Malpighian (germinative) layer** of dividing cells containing pigment (melanin).
- **Dermis** (middle layer): connective tissue containing the structures below.
- **Subcutaneous layer** (bottom): **fatty (adipose) tissue**.

Structures to show in the skin:

- A **hair** growing from a **hair follicle**, with its **hair root (bulb)** deep in the dermis and the hair shaft projecting above the skin;
- the **erector (arrector pili) muscle** attached to the hair follicle;
- a **sebaceous gland** opening into the hair follicle;
- a coiled **sweat gland** in the dermis, its **sweat duct** and the **sweat pore** at the surface;
- **blood capillaries** (and an arteriole and a venule) running through the dermis;
- **sensory receptors** (touch, pressure, pain, heat and cold) with a **nerve fibre**.

**Labels expected:** cornified layer, Malpighian layer, epidermis, dermis, hair shaft, hair follicle, hair root, erector muscle, sebaceous gland, sweat gland, sweat duct, sweat pore, blood capillary, arteriole, venule, sensory (touch) receptor, nerve fibre, fatty tissue (subcutaneous layer).
